BANKS["Robotics"] = [
{ t: "ros", d: 1, q: "In ROS, which communication mechanism is the best fit for continuously streaming sensor data such as laser scans?", o: ["A service, since each scan is a request", "An action, since scans deserve feedback", "A topic, using publish-subscribe", "A parameter updated at high rate"], a: 2, why: "Topics provide anonymous many-to-many publish-subscribe streaming. Services are request-response and actions are for long-running goals, not continuous data." },
{ t: "ros", d: 1, q: "Which ROS mechanism suits a one-off request-response interaction, such as asking a map server for the current map?", o: ["A service call", "A latched topic subscription", "An action with continuous feedback", "Replaying a rosbag"], a: 0, why: "Services implement synchronous request-response semantics, ideal for occasional queries that expect a single reply." },
{ t: "ros", d: 1, q: "A navigation goal runs for tens of seconds and you want progress feedback plus the ability to cancel. Which ROS interface fits best?", o: ["A parameter", "A topic", "A service", "An action"], a: 3, why: "Actions are designed for long-running, preemptable goals with periodic feedback and a final result." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "In a standard ROS tf tree, what does the transform from the odom frame to the base_link frame represent?", o: ["The fixed mounting offset between the odometry sensor and the base", "A continuous, smooth pose estimate from odometry that drifts over time", "The globally corrected pose that may jump when localization updates", "A static transform defined once in the URDF"], a: 1, why: "odom to base_link is continuous and smooth but drifts; global corrections are applied in the map to odom transform so local control never sees jumps." },
{ t: "ros", d: 1, q: "Which statement captures a key architectural difference between ROS 2 and ROS 1?", o: ["ROS 2 still requires a central roscore master to run", "ROS 2 uses DDS for discovery and transport, removing the central master", "ROS 2 dropped publish-subscribe messaging altogether, in favour of services", "ROS 2 nodes can only be written in Python"], a: 1, why: "ROS 2 is built on DDS middleware with peer-to-peer discovery, eliminating the single point of failure that roscore represented in ROS 1." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "Two ROS 2 nodes share a topic with matching name and type, yet no messages arrive. Which QoS combination is a classic cause?", o: ["Publisher and subscriber both set to reliable", "Publisher reliable, subscriber best effort", "Publisher and subscriber both set to best effort", "Publisher best effort, subscriber requesting reliable"], a: 3, why: "A subscriber requesting reliable cannot connect to a best-effort publisher; the offered QoS must meet the request. The other pairings are compatible." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "What is the primary value of recording a rosbag during a field test?", o: ["Timestamped topic data can be replayed offline to reproduce and debug issues", "It logs CPU and memory usage of every node", "It archives the compiled binaries used on the robot", "It snapshots the entire parameter configuration so that it can be restored later"], a: 0, why: "Bags record messages with timestamps so the exact data streams from a test can be replayed later against new code." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "What does a ROS launch file mainly give you over starting each node manually?", o: ["It compiles nodes in the correct order", "It guarantees real-time scheduling for all nodes", "One reproducible command starting many nodes with parameters and remappings", "It completely removes the need for any networking at all between the machines"], a: 2, why: "Launch files describe the whole system: which nodes start, their parameters, namespaces, and remappings, making bring-up reproducible." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "A teleop node and an autonomous planner both publish to the same velocity command topic. What happens by default?", o: ["Commands interleave and the robot follows whichever message arrived most recently", "ROS rejects the second publisher on the topic", "The subscriber simply averages both of the incoming command streams together for you", "The higher priority node wins automatically"], a: 0, why: "Topics allow multiple publishers with no built-in arbitration; the controller acts on the latest message, so an explicit mux or arbiter is needed." },
{ t: "ros", d: 2, q: "A lidar is bolted rigidly to the robot chassis. Where should the transform from base_link to the lidar frame come from?", o: ["The SLAM node should estimate it from each scan", "Integrated wheel odometry", "A static transform, typically from the URDF or a static transform publisher", "The lidar driver should fit it online from the observed motion of the point cloud"], a: 2, why: "Rigid sensor mounts are fixed geometry, so they belong in the URDF or a static publisher; estimating them online adds needless error." },
{ t: "ros", d: 3, q: "In ROS 2 with the default single-threaded executor, a slow lidar callback is running. What happens to the node's timer callbacks meanwhile?", o: ["They all run in parallel on whatever spare cores happen to exist, automatically", "They wait, because all callbacks share one thread and execute sequentially", "They pre-empt the lidar callback mid-execution", "They are silently discarded forever"], a: 1, why: "A single-threaded executor services callbacks one at a time, so a long callback delays timers; a multithreaded executor with callback groups enables concurrency." },
{ t: "ros", d: 3, q: "When replaying a bag to test localization, why run with simulated time (use_sim_time plus a clock publisher)?", o: ["It lets the bag replay faster than real time", "It disables all of the QoS compatibility checks for the whole duration of playback", "It decompresses the bag on the fly", "Nodes then stamp data and query tf against recorded time instead of wall clock"], a: 3, why: "With sim time, time lookups match the bag's timestamps, so tf queries and message synchronization behave as they did live." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 1, q: "What does forward kinematics compute for a robot arm?", o: ["The joint angles needed to reach a desired pose", "The joint torques required to hold a payload", "The motor currents for a commanded trajectory", "The end effector pose implied by given joint angles"], a: 3, why: "Forward kinematics maps joint space to task space. The reverse problem, pose to joint angles, is inverse kinematics." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 1, q: "How many degrees of freedom does an unconstrained rigid body have in 3D space?", o: ["Three", "Six: three translations and three rotations", "Four", "Nine, one per rotation matrix entry"], a: 1, why: "A free rigid body can translate along and rotate about three independent axes, giving six DOF in total." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "What does the manipulator Jacobian relate?", o: ["From the joint angles all the way through to the end effector pose", "Link masses to joint inertias", "Joint velocities to end effector linear and angular velocity", "Motor torque to motor current"], a: 2, why: "The Jacobian is the linear map from joint rates to end effector twist at the current pose; its transpose also maps tip forces to joint torques." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 1, q: "Why does inverse kinematics for a typical 6 DOF arm often have multiple valid solutions?", o: ["Distinct configurations, like elbow up and elbow down, reach the same pose", "The Jacobian is always singular somewhere", "Forward kinematics is not unique", "Floating point rounding is what creates all of these extra answers in practice"], a: 0, why: "Several joint configurations can place the tool at the identical pose, so IK solvers must pick among branches using limits, continuity, or cost." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "What happens to a manipulator at a kinematic singularity?", o: ["Its workspace temporarily expands", "It can no longer hold position against gravity", "It loses motion in some task-space direction and nearby joint speeds can spike", "Its inverse kinematics then becomes uniquely solvable right across the workspace"], a: 2, why: "At a singularity the Jacobian loses rank: some end effector directions become unreachable, and commanding motion near them demands enormous joint velocities." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "What is gimbal lock when using Euler angles?", o: ["The physical seizing up of a gimbal bearing while it is running at speed", "An overflow in angle wraparound at 180 degrees", "Loss of position accuracy at workspace edges", "Two rotation axes align at certain angles, losing one rotational DOF"], a: 3, why: "At specific angles, such as 90 degree pitch in many conventions, two Euler axes coincide, so one rotational DOF cannot be represented or controlled smoothly." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 1, q: "Why are unit quaternions widely preferred for representing orientation in robot software?", o: ["They need less memory than any other representation", "They avoid gimbal lock and interpolate smoothly", "They are the easiest form for humans to read", "They make all rotations commutative"], a: 1, why: "Quaternions represent orientation without singularities and support clean interpolation (slerp); Euler angles suffer gimbal lock and order ambiguity." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "Which property must a valid 3D rotation matrix satisfy?", o: ["Orthonormal rows and columns with determinant equal to plus one", "Determinant equal to zero", "Symmetry about the diagonal with trace equal to three", "All entries between zero and one"], a: 0, why: "Rotation matrices are special orthogonal: the transpose equals the inverse and the determinant is plus one, preserving lengths and handedness." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "What distinguishes the dexterous workspace of an arm from its reachable workspace?", o: ["Dexterous points can be reached with any tool orientation; reachable needs only one", "The dexterous workspace is always considerably larger than the whole reachable workspace", "The reachable workspace excludes points near singularities", "They differ only for arms with fewer than six joints"], a: 0, why: "The dexterous workspace is the subset of reachable points where full orientation freedom exists, so it is never larger than the reachable workspace." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 3, q: "Code compares quaternions componentwise, and a controller sometimes takes the long way around to a nearby orientation. What subtlety was missed?", o: ["Negating a quaternion simply inverts the whole direction of that rotation entirely", "q and minus q are the same rotation; fix the sign before comparing or blending", "The quaternions were not renormalized", "Euler conversion introduced gimbal lock"], a: 1, why: "Unit quaternions double cover rotations: q and minus q are physically identical. Checking the dot product sign and flipping one avoids long-way interpolation." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "A 7 DOF arm performing a 6 DOF pose task is kinematically redundant. What practical benefit does this give?", o: ["It doubles the payload capacity", "It removes all singularities from the workspace", "It makes the inverse kinematics into a single unique closed-form solution everywhere", "Null-space motion can avoid joint limits or obstacles while holding the pose"], a: 3, why: "Redundancy leaves a null space: internal motion that does not move the end effector, usable for limit avoidance or posture optimization. Singularities still exist." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 3, q: "Why use damped least squares rather than a plain Jacobian pseudoinverse for velocity-level IK near singularities?", o: ["It converges in fewer iterations everywhere", "It removes the singularity from the physical mechanism itself altogether and for good", "Damping bounds joint velocities near singularities at the cost of small task error", "It guarantees the globally shortest joint path"], a: 2, why: "Damped least squares trades a little tracking accuracy for bounded joint velocities where the pseudoinverse would blow up as singular values approach zero." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 2, q: "Two tools are given identical roll, pitch, yaw numbers yet produce different orientations. What is the most likely cause?", o: ["Gimbal lock corrupted one tool's maths", "They assume different rotation orders or intrinsic versus extrinsic conventions", "One tool secretly uses left-handed quaternions", "Trigonometric rounding differences between the various different maths libraries used"], a: 1, why: "Euler angles are meaningless without a convention: the axis order and intrinsic versus extrinsic composition change the resulting rotation." },
{ t: "kinematics", d: 3, q: "A manipulability ellipsoid at the current arm pose is long in one direction and nearly flat in another. What does this tell you?", o: ["The arm moves easily along the long axis but is near singular along the flat one", "The arm has simply reached one of its joint limits somewhere along the longest axis", "The workspace boundary lies in the flat direction", "The Jacobian is orthonormal at this pose"], a: 0, why: "Ellipsoid axes follow the Jacobian's singular values: a collapsing axis means little achievable velocity in that direction, indicating a nearby singularity." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 1, q: "For a differential drive robot to drive in a straight line, its two wheels must do what?", o: ["Spin in opposite directions at equal speed", "Spin with the left slightly faster", "Spin at equal speed in the same direction", "Hold one wheel stationary"], a: 2, why: "Equal wheel speeds give zero angular velocity, so the robot translates straight; any speed difference produces turning." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 2, q: "Viewed from above, you want a differential drive robot to rotate clockwise in place. How should the wheels move?", o: ["Both wheels forward at equal speed", "Both wheels backward at equal speed", "Left wheel backward, right wheel forward, equal speed", "Left wheel forward, right wheel backward, equal speed"], a: 3, why: "Opposite equal wheel speeds spin the robot about its centre; left forward with right backward yields clockwise rotation seen from above." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 1, q: "What makes a mecanum or omni-wheel robot holonomic in the plane?", o: ["It has more than two motors", "Its wheels never slip", "It weighs considerably less than any equivalent differential drive robot would do", "It can command x, y, and rotation independently, including pure sideways motion"], a: 3, why: "Holonomic means the controllable DOF equal the total planar DOF: the robot can translate in any direction while independently rotating." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 2, q: "Which statement describes the non-holonomic constraint of a differential drive robot?", o: ["It cannot rotate in place", "It can reach considerably fewer poses than an equivalent holonomic robot ever could", "It cannot move sideways instantaneously, though it can still reach any planar pose", "It must always follow straight line segments"], a: 2, why: "The rolling constraint blocks lateral velocity at any instant, but combined turns and drives still reach any pose: the constraint is on velocity, not reachability." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 2, q: "What is a common drawback of omni or mecanum drives compared to conventional wheels?", o: ["Roller slip is part of normal operation, hurting traction, efficiency, and odometry", "They simply cannot drive forwards anything like as fast under any kind of load at all", "They require twice as many encoders per wheel", "They only work on perfectly smooth glass"], a: 0, why: "Omni-style wheels rely on passive rollers that slip by design, trading traction and odometry accuracy for maneuverability, especially on dust or soft ground." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 1, q: "For a legged robot walking with a statically stable gait, what must remain true throughout the gait?", o: ["At least one foot is airborne at all times", "The vertical projection of the centre of mass stays inside the support polygon", "The torso remains perfectly level", "All of the feet remain in contact with the ground continuously throughout the gait"], a: 1, why: "Static stability requires the CoM projection to remain within the polygon formed by the stance feet, so the robot balances even if paused mid-stride." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 2, q: "Why does a trot gait challenge static balance on a quadruped?", o: ["All four of the feet leave the ground at precisely the same moment in every stride", "The gait doubles the stance width", "Support shrinks to two diagonal feet, nearly a line, so balance becomes dynamic", "The front legs carry no load in a trot"], a: 2, why: "With only a diagonal pair in stance, the support polygon degenerates toward a line under the body, so the robot must rely on dynamic rather than static balance." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 3, q: "A differential drive robot has wheel radius 0.05 m and track width 0.4 m. Both wheels spin at 10 rad per s but in opposite directions. What is the motion?", o: ["Pure rotation in place at about 2.5 rad per s", "Pure rotation in place at about 5 rad per s", "Straight line motion at 0.5 m per s", "Rotation about the left wheel at 1.25 rad per s"], a: 0, why: "Each rim moves at 0.5 m per s. Opposite directions give zero linear velocity; yaw rate is the rim speed difference over the track: 1.0 divided by 0.4 is 2.5 rad per s." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 2, q: "What is the static stability margin of a legged robot?", o: ["The height of the centre of mass above the ground", "The maximum torque reserve of the leg actuators", "The amount of battery capacity that is kept back in reserve for balancing purposes", "The smallest distance from the CoM ground projection to the support polygon edge"], a: 3, why: "The margin quantifies how far the CoM projection can shift before leaving the support polygon; larger margins tolerate bigger disturbances." },
{ t: "locomotion", d: 3, q: "In dynamic walking, keeping the zero moment point inside the support polygon guarantees what, exactly?", o: ["The robot simply cannot fall over at all, even under any external pushes", "The stance foot does not tip; contact forces can balance the moments", "The centre of mass stays directly over the feet", "Energy consumption stays minimal"], a: 1, why: "ZMP inside the polygon means the foot will not rotate about its edge. It does not by itself prevent falls from pushes or slip, and the CoM may leave the polygon." },
{ t: "perception", d: 1, q: "In the pinhole camera model, the image size of an object is proportional to what?", o: ["Focal length multiplied by object distance", "Focal length divided by object distance", "Object distance divided by focal length", "Neither focal length nor distance"], a: 1, why: "Projection scales by focal length over depth: longer focal lengths magnify, and doubling the distance halves the image size." },
{ t: "perception", d: 1, q: "Which parameters are the extrinsics of a camera?", o: ["Focal length and principal point", "Lens distortion coefficients", "The sensor resolution together with the physical pixel pitch used", "The rotation and translation of the camera relative to an external frame"], a: 3, why: "Extrinsics locate the camera in the world or robot frame; focal length, principal point, and distortion belong to the intrinsics." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "Why is a checkerboard target the classic tool for camera calibration?", o: ["Its known geometry gives precisely detectable corners to solve intrinsics and distortion", "It reflects the infrared light entirely uniformly, so that any depth camera can see it well", "It maximizes image brightness during capture", "It is required by the pinhole model itself"], a: 0, why: "Corner points with known relative positions provide many accurate 2D to 3D correspondences, constraining the camera model parameters." },
{ t: "perception", d: 1, q: "How does a stereo camera pair estimate depth?", o: ["By timing the flight of emitted light pulses", "By comparing focus blur between the two lenses", "From pixel disparity between matched features; closer objects show larger disparity", "From the brightness differences between each of the two separate camera images taken"], a: 2, why: "Depth is triangulated from disparity: depth equals focal length times baseline divided by disparity, so disparity shrinks with distance." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "What is the effect of widening the baseline of a stereo camera rig?", o: ["Better depth precision at long range, but a larger minimum range and harder matching", "Uniformly better depth at every possible distance, and with no trade-offs of any kind at all", "Lower depth precision everywhere", "It only increases the field of view"], a: 0, why: "A wider baseline increases disparity for distant points, improving far-range precision, but near objects fall outside both views and appearance differences grow." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "For outdoor navigation in sunlight, why is lidar often chosen over an active IR structured-light depth camera?", o: ["Lidar units are always cheaper", "Lidar also captures the colour of the entire scene as well as capturing all its depth", "Sunlight swamps the camera's projected IR pattern, while lidar still ranges reliably", "Lidar consumes far less power"], a: 2, why: "Structured-light sensors rely on seeing their own IR pattern, which sunlight washes out; lidar pulses remain detectable and give long, accurate range." },
{ t: "perception", d: 1, q: "What do fiducial markers such as AprilTags provide to a robot vision system?", o: ["Dense depth across the whole scene", "Known landmarks whose identity and full 6 DOF pose come from one camera image", "Fully automatic intrinsic calibration without needing a target of any kind at all", "Compression of the video stream"], a: 1, why: "Because tag geometry and size are known, detecting one yields its ID and relative pose, useful for ground truth, docking, and calibration checks." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "What does optical flow measure?", o: ["Metric depth for every pixel", "The intrinsic parameters belonging to the camera itself", "The identity of moving objects", "The apparent motion of image brightness patterns between frames"], a: 3, why: "Optical flow is the 2D motion field of image intensities; combining it with geometry or depth is required to infer 3D motion." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "How does a time-of-flight depth sensor measure range?", o: ["By triangulating a projected dot pattern", "By measuring only the reflected signal amplitude", "By stereo matching between two imagers", "From the round trip time or phase shift of emitted light"], a: 3, why: "ToF sensors emit modulated light and convert its return delay or phase shift into distance, independent of scene texture." },
{ t: "perception", d: 3, q: "During fast rotation, a robot's camera shows vertical poles leaning diagonally in each frame. The most likely cause is what?", o: ["Barrel lens distortion", "Rolling shutter exposing image rows at slightly different times", "An incorrect value used for the camera's principal point", "Ordinary motion blur"], a: 1, why: "Rolling shutter sensors read rows sequentially, so fast motion skews rigid structures. Blur smears edges rather than slanting them, and distortion is static." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "Why does passive stereo fail on a blank white wall while an active pattern-projecting depth camera copes?", o: ["The wall absorbs all infrared light", "Stereo needs two entirely separate exposures, taken at two different moments in time", "Matching needs texture; the projector supplies a pattern where the scene has none", "Active cameras have wider baselines"], a: 2, why: "Stereo correspondence is ambiguous without texture. Projecting a pattern manufactures features, which is exactly what active stereo and structured light exploit." },
{ t: "perception", d: 3, q: "Lidar points reprojected into a camera land a few pixels off, with error growing toward the image corners. What is the best first suspect?", o: ["Uncorrected or poorly fitted lens distortion coefficients", "A large error in the extrinsic translation", "A time offset between lidar and camera", "Random sensor noise"], a: 0, why: "Distortion grows with distance from the image centre, matching the corner-heavy error. Extrinsic or timing errors would not specifically worsen at the edges." },
{ t: "perception", d: 2, q: "Classic optical flow methods such as Lucas-Kanade rest on which core assumption?", o: ["Constant scene depth across the image", "A stationary camera", "Brightness constancy with small motion between frames", "Known camera extrinsics"], a: 2, why: "They assume a pixel keeps its intensity as it moves slightly between frames, which linearizes the problem; larger motions need pyramids or other tricks." },
{ t: "perception", d: 3, q: "A spinning lidar on a fast-moving robot renders straight corridors as curved or skewed. What is the standard remedy?", o: ["Increase the spin rate and simply accept having fewer points", "Average several consecutive scans together", "Recalibrate the lidar intrinsics", "Motion-compensate each point using IMU or odometry across the sweep"], a: 3, why: "Points in one sweep are captured over the whole rotation period while the robot moves; de-skewing transforms each point by the pose at its own timestamp." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 1, q: "Why does wheel odometry accumulate error without bound over distance?", o: ["Encoders are simply designed to lose some of their counts at high speed", "Small slip and measurement errors are integrated and never corrected", "The wheels physically shrink with wear", "Odometry only works for holonomic robots"], a: 1, why: "Dead reckoning integrates velocity estimates, so every small error in slip, wheel radius, or track width compounds into growing pose drift." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 1, q: "What does each cell in an occupancy grid map represent?", o: ["The probability that the corresponding space contains an obstacle", "The colour of the floor at that location", "The number of times that the robot has actually visited that particular cell", "The Wi-Fi signal strength at that point"], a: 0, why: "Occupancy grids discretize space and store per-cell occupancy probability, typically updated with log-odds from range sensor data." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "For global localization in a known map, what key advantage does a particle filter hold over an EKF?", o: ["It represents multi-modal beliefs, tracking several distinct pose hypotheses", "It always needs less computation", "It requires no motion model", "It guarantees the statistically optimal estimate in absolutely every single case"], a: 0, why: "Particles can cover many hypotheses at once, essential when the robot could be in several similar-looking places; an EKF maintains a single Gaussian and cannot." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 1, q: "In SLAM, what is loop closure?", o: ["Completing one full lap of a planned patrol route", "Recognizing a previously mapped place and correcting accumulated drift against it", "Closing the whole of the control loop between the planner and the motors themselves", "Deleting old map data to save memory"], a: 1, why: "Detecting a revisited location adds a constraint between distant poses, letting the optimizer pull the drifted trajectory and map back into consistency." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "In a navigation costmap, what is the purpose of inflating obstacles?", o: ["To make the map file smaller", "To simulate the obstacles gradually growing larger over time throughout the map", "To hide sensor noise from the planner", "To add a clearance buffer around obstacles reflecting the robot's footprint"], a: 3, why: "Inflation spreads cost outward from obstacles so paths keep a safe margin, effectively letting a point-sized planner respect the robot's real size." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "How does A star improve on Dijkstra for goal-directed grid planning?", o: ["It ignores traversal costs to run faster", "It searches backward from the goal only", "An admissible heuristic biases expansion toward the goal, visiting fewer cells", "It always finds strictly shorter paths than Dijkstra itself would ever manage to find"], a: 2, why: "A star adds a heuristic lower bound on remaining cost. With an admissible heuristic the result is still optimal, typically with far fewer node expansions." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 1, q: "What distinguishes SLAM from localization?", o: ["SLAM runs only in simulation", "SLAM estimates the map and robot pose together; localization assumes a prior map", "Localization needs no sensors", "SLAM simply does not estimate the pose of the robot itself in any way at all whatsoever"], a: 1, why: "Localization matches sensing against an existing map; SLAM must build that map while simultaneously tracking the pose within it." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "When would you reach for a sampling-based planner like RRT instead of grid A star?", o: ["High-dimensional spaces such as arm motion planning where grids explode", "Planar navigation on a comparatively small two-dimensional occupancy grid", "When a provably optimal path is mandatory", "When the map is a perfect maze of corridors"], a: 0, why: "Grid search scales exponentially with dimensions. Sampling planners probe continuous high-DOF spaces efficiently, at the price of optimality guarantees." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "A particle filter localizer has all particles tightly clustered on a pose that is visibly wrong. What has happened?", o: ["The filter is working perfectly fine, and so it must be the map that is wrong here", "Too many particles were allocated", "Particle deprivation: diversity collapsed and the filter cannot recover alone", "The sensor model is too pessimistic"], a: 2, why: "Resampling can concentrate particles on a wrong hypothesis and discard the true one. Recovery needs injected random particles or reinitialization." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 3, q: "After a loop closure, why does a pose graph optimizer adjust the entire trajectory instead of only snapping the latest pose?", o: ["Because the last pose is the only one that has no accumulated error left in it", "To keep computation time constant", "Because older poses are locked by earlier scans", "Error accumulated along the path; spreading the fix keeps all constraints consistent"], a: 3, why: "Every pose carries a share of accumulated drift. Graph optimization minimizes total constraint error, deforming the whole trajectory into global consistency." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 2, q: "In the ROS navigation convention, why does the localizer publish the map to odom transform rather than map to base_link directly?", o: ["Because tf cannot chain more than two frames", "Because the odometry is far more accurate than the localisation could ever possibly be", "To save bandwidth on the transform topic", "So control sees smooth odometry while corrections shift the odom frame without jumps"], a: 3, why: "Composing map to odom with odom to base_link keeps the robot's local frame continuous; localization jumps are absorbed upstream where they cannot jerk the controller." },
{ t: "slam-nav", d: 3, q: "If the heuristic used by A star overestimates the true remaining cost, what is the consequence?", o: ["The search becomes invalid and returns nothing", "Memory use grows without bound", "The returned path may be suboptimal, though the search often finishes faster", "Nothing changes at all, because the heuristic never affects the result"], a: 2, why: "Admissibility, never overestimating, is what guarantees optimality. Inflated heuristics, as in weighted A star, deliberately trade path quality for speed." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 1, q: "What does an H-bridge enable for a brushed DC motor?", o: ["Driving the motor in both directions and braking, using four switching elements", "Doubling the motor's rated voltage", "Permanently converting the motor into a generator rather than leaving it as a motor", "Measuring rotor position without sensors"], a: 0, why: "The four-switch bridge can apply supply voltage in either polarity across the motor, giving forward, reverse, coast, and brake modes." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 1, q: "How does PWM control the speed of a brushed DC motor?", o: ["The switching frequency itself sets the speed", "Rapid on-off switching makes average voltage track duty cycle, setting speed", "It varies the strength of the permanent magnets inside the rotor of the machine", "It changes the number of active windings"], a: 1, why: "The motor's inductance and inertia smooth the switched voltage, so the mechanical response follows the duty-cycle-weighted average, not the switching frequency." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "Why do brushless DC motors generally outlast brushed motors?", o: ["Their bearings are always ceramic", "They run considerably cooler under any load at all, simply by definition", "Commutation is electronic, eliminating brush and commutator wear", "They contain no moving parts at all"], a: 2, why: "Brushes are a wearing, arcing sliding contact. BLDC motors commutate electronically using rotor position sensing, leaving bearings as the main wear item." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 1, q: "What characterizes a stepper motor driven open loop?", o: ["It moves a fixed angle per pulse and holds position without feedback", "It requires an encoder to move at all", "Its speed is entirely independent of whatever the applied pulse rate is", "It cannot hold a stationary load"], a: 0, why: "Steppers advance in discrete steps per input pulse and produce holding torque at standstill, which is why many machines use them without encoders." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "An open loop stepper axis ends up in the wrong position after aggressive moves under load, with no error reported. Why?", o: ["The driver automatically re-zeroed the axis", "Electrical noise has somehow reversed the direction of the stepping altogether", "The lead screw changed pitch under load", "It missed steps when demanded torque exceeded available torque, undetected"], a: 3, why: "Steppers stall silently when overloaded, losing steps. Without feedback the assumed position diverges from reality; hence homing switches or closed loop steppers." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "An ideal 30 to 1 reduction gearbox multiplies motor torque by 30. What happens to output speed, and why?", o: ["Speed is also multiplied by 30", "Speed is divided by 30, since power in roughly equals power out", "Speed is unchanged; only torque changes", "The speed is simply divided by the square root of 30 instead"], a: 1, why: "A gearbox trades speed for torque: with power conserved minus losses, torque times speed stays constant, so a 30x torque gain means a 30x speed reduction." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 1, q: "On a standard hobby servo, what does the input pulse width command?", o: ["The output speed of the shaft", "The target angular position of the output shaft", "The maximum current the servo may draw", "The PWM frequency of the internal motor drive"], a: 1, why: "Classic RC servos map pulse width, roughly 1 to 2 ms, to shaft angle; an internal loop drives the motor until the feedback potentiometer matches the command." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "How does gearbox backlash typically manifest in a robot joint under closed loop position control?", o: ["A constant offset in one direction only", "Higher top speed than specified", "A perfectly smooth but rather slow response in both of the directions of travel alike", "A dead zone on direction reversal, causing error and possible limit-cycle chatter"], a: 3, why: "Backlash is lost motion between gear teeth: on reversal the output stands still until teeth re-engage, degrading accuracy and destabilizing tight loops." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "Why does a joint with only an incremental encoder need a homing routine at power-up?", o: ["Incremental encoders report change only, so absolute position is unknown at start", "Incremental encoders drift with temperature", "The homing move is what charges up the internal backup battery inside the encoder itself", "The index pulse erases the count at startup"], a: 0, why: "Incremental encoders count pulses relative to wherever the joint woke up. A home switch or index mark establishes the absolute reference; absolute encoders avoid this." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 3, q: "Commanding a hard deceleration, a robot's motor driver faults with DC bus overvoltage. What is happening?", o: ["The battery voltage must have sagged below the low-voltage cutoff threshold somewhere", "The PWM frequency was set too high", "Regeneration: the decelerating motor pumps energy back and raises the bus voltage", "The current sensor is miscalibrated"], a: 2, why: "A braking motor acts as a generator. Unless the battery can absorb the charge or a brake resistor dissipates it, regenerated energy raises the bus until protection trips." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 2, q: "A quadrature encoder disc has 500 lines. With full 4x quadrature decoding, how many counts per revolution do you get?", o: ["2000", "500", "1000", "8000"], a: 0, why: "Quadrature gives two channels 90 degrees apart; counting every edge of both channels yields four counts per line, so 500 lines become 2000 counts." },
{ t: "actuators", d: 3, q: "Motor A has twice the Kv (speed constant) of motor B, all else comparable. At equal current, what torque does A produce?", o: ["The same torque as B", "About twice the torque of B", "About half the torque of B", "About one quarter the torque of B"], a: 2, why: "Torque constant is inversely proportional to Kv in consistent units, so doubling Kv halves torque per amp: high Kv motors spin faster but need gearing for torque." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 1, q: "Why can you not get usable position by double integrating a consumer-grade accelerometer for more than a few seconds?", o: ["Accelerometers cannot sample fast enough", "Bias and noise integrate twice, so position error grows quadratically or worse", "Gravity cannot be measured by accelerometers", "Position estimation always needs at least three separate sensors in order to work"], a: 1, why: "A constant accelerometer bias becomes a linearly growing velocity error and a quadratically growing position error, which is why INS needs aiding sensors." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 1, q: "How does a complementary filter combine gyro and accelerometer data for tilt estimation?", o: ["It averages the two raw signals equally", "It uses the accelerometer only when the gyro happens to have saturated", "It multiplies the two estimates together", "It trusts the gyro at high frequency and the accelerometer at low frequency"], a: 3, why: "The gyro is smooth short-term but drifts; the accelerometer is noisy but unbiased long-term. High-pass plus low-pass blending exploits the best of each." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 2, q: "What symptom does an uncorrected gyroscope bias produce in an attitude estimate?", o: ["Extra white noise but zero mean error", "No effect while the robot is stationary", "A scale error proportional to rotation speed", "Steady angular drift at a roughly constant rate, even at rest"], a: 3, why: "Integrating a rate signal with a constant offset yields a steadily ramping angle; estimating and subtracting the bias, as Kalman-based filters do, is essential." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 2, q: "Why is a magnetometer often a poor heading reference on an indoor mobile robot?", o: ["Earth's field vanishes inside buildings", "Motors, currents, and steel distort the field (hard and soft iron effects)", "Magnetometers only ever work above 1 kHz sample rates in any case whatsoever", "Heading requires GPS lock to initialize"], a: 1, why: "Nearby ferromagnetic material and currents bend and offset the local field. Calibration handles static effects, but time-varying distortions still corrupt heading." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 2, q: "What does an EKF offer over a fixed-gain complementary filter for IMU fusion?", o: ["It completely removes the need for there being a gyro of any kind at all whatsoever", "It is always cheaper to compute", "Uncertainty-weighted corrections plus estimation of extra states like gyro bias", "It never diverges under any conditions"], a: 2, why: "The EKF adapts its gain from modelled covariances and can jointly estimate biases and other states, at the cost of more computation and tuning." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 3, q: "An EKF fuses wheel odometry with a gyro. During wheel spin on gravel the pose estimate veers badly. What is a principled fix?", o: ["Detect slip from wheel versus inertial disagreement and inflate odometry covariance", "Delete the gyro from the filter", "Simply double the process noise permanently, and then just leave it well alone for good", "Lower the filter update rate"], a: 0, why: "Slip makes odometry temporarily untrustworthy. Detecting the inconsistency and de-weighting odometry during slip keeps the filter honest without discarding it." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 2, q: "A stationary accelerometer reads about 9.8 m per s squared pointing up. Why is the reading not zero?", o: ["It is measuring the Coriolis effect", "The device is miscalibrated by exactly gravity", "It measures specific force: the support reaction, not coordinate acceleration", "Environmental vibration just happens to add up to exactly gravity itself"], a: 2, why: "Accelerometers sense specific force. At rest the case pushes the proof mass up against gravity, reading plus one g; in free fall the reading is zero." },
{ t: "estimation", d: 3, q: "Why can an EKF diverge if initialized with a badly wrong heading?", o: ["Kalman filters forbid nonzero initial error", "The gyro simply cannot spin around fast enough to be able to correct for any of it at all", "Covariance always shrinks too slowly to matter", "Linearizing around a wrong state gives bad Jacobians, pushing the estimate further off"], a: 3, why: "The EKF is only valid near its linearization point. With a large initial heading error the Jacobians misdirect corrections, which can compound instead of converging." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 1, q: "What defines a hard real-time requirement in a robot control system?", o: ["The code must run on a fast CPU", "Missing a deadline constitutes a failure, however good the average latency is", "The control loop must always be run at 1 kHz or above, no matter what else happens", "All code must be written in C"], a: 1, why: "Hard real-time is about guaranteed worst-case timing, not raw speed: a control or safety deadline missed even rarely is a system failure." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 1, q: "What is the role of a watchdog timer in robot firmware?", o: ["It resets or safes the system if software stops refreshing it, catching hangs", "It measures how long each function takes", "It schedules tasks in priority order", "It synchronises every one of the clocks right across the whole of the robot network"], a: 0, why: "Firmware must periodically service the watchdog; if a crash or lockup stops that, the hardware forces a reset or a safe state autonomously." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 2, q: "Why pair a microcontroller running control loops with a separate Linux computer for planning in one robot?", o: ["The MCU gives deterministic timing for control; Linux handles heavy non-real-time work", "Linux cannot talk to sensors at all", "Microcontrollers are simply a great deal faster than any desktop CPU could ever hope to be", "It avoids having to write any firmware"], a: 0, why: "Standard Linux offers no hard timing guarantees, so tight motor loops live on an MCU or RTOS, while perception and planning use Linux's compute and ecosystem." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 2, q: "What is the correct implementation of a robot's emergency stop?", o: ["A subscriber that stops the motors when a stop message arrives", "A hardware circuit that cuts actuator power independent of all software", "A prominent red button in the web dashboard", "A network command retried until acknowledged"], a: 1, why: "An e-stop must work when software has failed, so it belongs in hardware, physically interrupting actuator power or drive enables. Software stops only complement it." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 1, q: "A teleoperated robot loses its radio link mid-drive. What should its firmware do by default?", o: ["Keep on executing whatever the last received command happened to be, indefinitely", "Accelerate back toward the operator", "Reboot the main computer immediately", "Detect stale commands via timeout and bring the robot to a controlled safe stop"], a: 3, why: "Command streams need an implicit deadman: when messages stop arriving, the safe default is a controlled stop, not continuing blind on stale data." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 2, q: "Teleop, autonomy, and a safety monitor can all command the robot base. What is the standard structure?", o: ["Let all three of them publish, and hope that the timing works out", "Run each source on a different day of testing", "A priority-based command multiplexer, with safety at the highest priority", "Average the three command streams"], a: 2, why: "A command mux or arbiter admits exactly one source at a time by priority, so safety and teleop can always override autonomy predictably." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 2, q: "Why should telemetry from all robot subsystems be timestamped against a common or synchronized clock?", o: ["It compresses the resulting log files a great deal more efficiently as well", "Events across subsystems can then be correlated when reconstructing faults", "Regulations require synchronized logs", "It makes the logs human readable"], a: 1, why: "Fault diagnosis usually hinges on ordering: knowing whether a current spike preceded the watchdog reset requires comparable timestamps across sources." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 3, q: "A high priority control task misses deadlines whenever a low priority logger holds a shared mutex while a medium task runs. Name the problem and a fix.", o: ["Priority inversion; enable priority inheritance on the mutex", "Deadlock; add a second mutex", "Starvation; lower the control task priority", "Jitter; buy a faster processor"], a: 0, why: "The medium task pre-empts the low task holding the lock, indirectly blocking the high task. Priority inheritance boosts the holder so it releases quickly." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 2, q: "What does a windowed watchdog catch that a simple timeout watchdog does not?", o: ["Power supply brownouts", "Slow memory leaks", "A runaway loop that refreshes the watchdog too fast as well as too slow", "Stack overflows occurring somewhere inside the interrupt handlers themselves"], a: 2, why: "A windowed watchdog requires refresh within a time window: servicing too early also triggers it, catching code stuck in a tight loop that still pets the watchdog." },
{ t: "architecture", d: 3, q: "A 1 kHz balance loop on desktop Linux occasionally stutters; logs show loop periods spiking to several milliseconds. Root cause and remedy?", o: ["Insufficient RAM; add swap space", "A tf tree error; rebuild the URDF", "Compiler optimisations, and so they ought simply to be disabled altogether for now", "OS scheduling jitter; move the loop to an MCU, RTOS, or tuned real-time kernel"], a: 3, why: "Stock Linux gives no bounded scheduling latency, so tight loops stall under load. Real-time kernels with priorities, or offloading to an MCU, restore determinism." },
{ t: "sim", d: 1, q: "What does a URDF file describe?", o: ["A compiled motion plan for the robot", "The physical layout of the network running between every one of the robot's computers", "The robot's software dependency graph", "The robot's links, joints, geometry, and inertial properties as a kinematic tree"], a: 3, why: "URDF is an XML robot description covering kinematics, visual and collision geometry, and inertials, consumed by tf, visualization tools, and simulators." },
{ t: "sim", d: 1, q: "Why are legged locomotion policies typically trained in simulation instead of directly on the physical robot?", o: ["Simulators model contact perfectly", "Real robots cannot run RL code onboard", "Sim allows millions of safe, parallel, faster than real time trials without wear", "Policies that are trained on the real hardware simply cannot ever be saved anywhere"], a: 2, why: "RL needs vast experience including falls. Parallel simulation delivers that cheaply and safely, then transfer techniques bridge the remaining gap to hardware." },
{ t: "sim", d: 2, q: "What is domain randomization?", o: ["Varying sim parameters like friction, mass, and latency so the policy generalizes", "Shuffling training data order between epochs", "Random restarts of the whole physics engine, in order to avoid any crashes", "Randomizing the neural network weights each episode"], a: 0, why: "By training across many perturbed versions of the simulator, the policy treats the real world as just another sample from the training distribution." },
{ t: "sim", d: 2, q: "Which of these is a common, major contributor to the sim-to-real gap for legged robots?", o: ["Using quaternions in the simulator state", "Unmodelled actuator behaviour: torque limits, friction, backlash, and latency", "Having far too many CPU cores available throughout the whole of the training run", "Logging observations at full rate"], a: 1, why: "Idealized sim actuators respond instantly and linearly; real motors and gearboxes do not. Actuator modelling is consistently a dominant gap source." },
{ t: "sim", d: 2, q: "Why do rigid body physics engines struggle to reproduce real-world contact exactly?", o: ["They cannot simulate gravity accurately", "Floating point maths simply forbids collision detection of any sort whatsoever", "Contact and friction rely on approximate penalty or constraint solver models", "Rigid body dynamics has no known equations"], a: 2, why: "Impact, stick-slip friction, and compliance are stiff, discontinuous phenomena; solvers approximate them, so contact-rich behaviour diverges from reality most." },
{ t: "sim", d: 3, q: "A policy walks perfectly in sim but oscillates and falls on the real quadruped. Which mitigation most directly targets the likely cause?", o: ["Model actuator latency and torque limits in sim and randomize them during training", "Simply train for a great many more epochs in exactly the same simulator all over again", "Use a larger network with the same simulator", "Raise the joint controller gains on the hardware"], a: 0, why: "Oscillation on hardware usually traces to unmodelled actuator dynamics and delays. Modelling and randomizing them attacks the gap; more training in a wrong sim overfits it." },
{ t: "sim", d: 2, q: "Why do incorrect inertial values in a URDF matter even when the robot model looks visually correct?", o: ["They change the mesh file checksums", "They break tf frame naming", "Simulators will always simply refuse to load any sort of incorrect inertia values whatsoever", "Physics and model-based control depend on mass and inertia, so dynamics come out wrong"], a: 3, why: "Visual meshes are cosmetic; dynamics engines and controllers consume the inertial tags. Wrong values distort accelerations, contacts, and computed-torque control." },
{ t: "sim", d: 3, q: "Why can overly wide domain randomization ranges hurt a sim-trained policy?", o: ["Wide ranges make the simulator run slower", "The policy turns conservative, sacrificing performance to survive implausible dynamics", "Randomization prevents the use of GPUs", "Wide ranges will always guarantee overfitting towards one extreme end of the whole range"], a: 1, why: "Randomization trades optimality for robustness. If ranges cover dynamics the real robot never exhibits, the policy hedges against them and underperforms." }
];
